#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Owner-aware GitHub App credential helper.

Interpreter is pinned to the system python3, NOT `env python3`: git invokes a credential
helper with the caller's environment, so under any activated virtualenv (a uv run, an agent
shell, a project venv) `env python3` can resolve to an interpreter the helper was never run
under. It needs nothing past the standard library, so it runs isolated (-I): a PYTHONPATH,
PYTHONSTARTUP or user site in that environment changes nothing here.

Git credential-helper usage (wired up in gh-app-routes.gitconfig):
  gh-app-token <profile> get

Direct usage prints a raw installation token:
  gh-app-token <profile> [--owner <login>] [--repo <name>]... [--read-only]

A returned installation token comes from a cache and may have as little as five minutes
left (TOKEN_REFRESH_MARGIN). A caller that holds it longer sets GH_APP_TOKEN_MIN_LIFETIME
to the seconds it needs: a cached token with less life left than that is not reused, and a
fresh broker release (an hour) is returned instead. `knives gh` sets it for each gh call it
runs, because gh reads its token once and keeps it for the whole call: a `gh run watch`
handed a cached token with twelve minutes left failed with 401 Bad credentials twelve
minutes in (2026-10-22). A fresh release is returned whatever the request: GitHub issues an
installation token for one hour, so asking for an hour (as a watch does) means a fresh token,
never a loop, and a caller needing more than that hour asks again.

A cached token is also asked of GitHub (GET /rate_limit, which spends none of the rate limit)
once its last check is LIVENESS_INTERVAL (60 s) old. A 401 there means GitHub revoked the
token: its cache entry is dropped and one fresh token is minted in its place. Any other answer,
or none, records the check and serves the cached token. So a token revoked while cached, as
after a leak, is served for at most a minute. On 2026-11-22 a leaked token was revoked a minute
after it was minted, and every agent-profile call for its repository on that machine got the
dead token and a 401 until the cache entry was replaced by hand.

A profile lives in git config and needs the App id (which names its covered-owner file) and
the secrets-broker secret holding the App's key; a fallback secret is optional:
  [gh-app "<profile>"]
      app-id          = ...
      broker-secret   = GITHUB_APP_<PERSON>  ; a github-app secret of the secrets broker
      fallback-secret = NAME_OF_SECRET       ; a personal access token, from `secrets get`
      key-secret      = NAME_OF_SECRET       ; this machine only: mint from secretsd (below)
      refuse-owner    = LOGIN                ; this machine only, one value per owner (below)

A machine refuses an owner for a profile by naming it in refuse-owner, in its own
`~/.config/git/config`. Only that file counts, whatever XDG_CONFIG_HOME is; ~/.gitconfig and a
repository's config do not. For a refused owner the helper reads and seeds no cache, mints no
App token and falls back to no personal token: it prints one line on stderr, prints `quit=1` on
stdout in credential mode, logs `refused` in decisions.log and exits 1. An owner-wide broker
release minted for another owner caches no token for a refused owner. With no
`~/.config/git/config`, no owner is refused. A dangling link at that path, a file this process
may not read, an unsearchable ~/.config or ~/.config/git, and a file git cannot parse each
refuse every owner. git's stderr is not read: GIT_TRACE and a git wrapper on PATH write there.
refuse-owner is multi-valued and matches without regard to case. Add each owner with
  git config --file ~/.config/git/config --add gh-app.<profile>.refuse-owner <login>
oryx refuses trajectory-labs-pbc for the agent profile (HOME-216).

A machine where the agent-secrets client finds no identity (no launcher key, no person's
sign-in, no helper session, no machine identity) gets every mint refused. Such a machine opts in
to minting here by setting key-secret in its own git config, `~/.config/git/config`, which no
other machine reads, to the secretsd secret holding the App's private key (base64-encoded
PEM), kept in that machine's gitignored secrets.local.env:
  git config --file ~/.config/git/config gh-app.<profile>.key-secret <NAME>
With key-secret set, a cache miss reads that key, signs an App JWT with openssl (the key goes
to it through a pipe, never argv or a file), lists the App's installations and mints the
owner's token, narrowed as asked; the broker is never asked. Every rule below holds the same
way, with the App's installation list in place of the broker's release.

Without key-secret the helper never holds the App's key. On a cache miss it asks the secrets
broker through the broker's client, agent-secrets, which mints the installation tokens:
  agent-secrets GITHUB_APP_<PERSON> --reason "gh-app-token <owner>" -- printenv GITHUB_APP_<PERSON>
and reads the release from its stdout:
  {"installations": {"<owner>": {"installation_id": <int>, "token": "...",
  "expires_at": "<RFC 3339>"}}}
Every owner's token in a release is cached, and every owner and installation id is merged
into a covered-owner file that never shrinks.

The client needs no registered session: outside one it acts as the person signed in on this
machine, or as the machine itself (cybermonklabs/legion packages/envoy/cmd/cipher/chain.go).
That serves the callers no list can keep: the lane relays, units such as ossv-ops-sync, the
speed services, and any job an agent detached, which systemd --user adopts once the tool call
that started it exits, so it no longer descends from its session's registered root. The client
also needs AGENT_SECRETS_URL, which omp's eval kernel and such units do not pass on, so it runs
after agent-secrets/broker-url.sh, which loads broker.env's URL when the caller has none.

In an agent box the broker identity arrives after the box does, so a request waits while the
launcher's enrollment.pending marker says one is still under way, up to 300 s
(ENROLLMENT_WAIT_SECONDS). The marker is looked for in the key dir the agent-secrets client
reads: AGENT_SECRETS_KEY_DIR, else $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/agent-secrets, else
/run/user/<uid>/agent-secrets. Nowhere else is there such a marker, so nothing else waits.

--repo and --read-only narrow the token of one owner (INFRA-322). This helper passes the client
--target <owner>, or --target <owner>/<repo> once for each --repo. --read-only asks for the
profile's read secret, its broker-secret with _READ appended (GITHUB_APP_<PERSON>_READ, which
holds contents:read and metadata:read), in place of broker-secret itself (GITHUB_APP_<PERSON>,
which holds the App's own permissions): the broker serves each as a named profile and mints the
narrowed token with GitHub's access_tokens body. A narrowed token has its own cache entry, so it
never reuses or replaces the owner-wide one, and it never falls back to the personal token,
which cannot be narrowed.

The target owner comes from --owner, then GH_APP_OWNER, then the owner segment of the
credential request's path (requires credential.useHttpPath). Whether an owner is covered is
decided from the broker's release and the covered-owner file alone, never from a failed
request:

- Owner in the release: answer with its token. Any broker failure (a pending or denied
  request, an error, a release that does not parse) emits `quit=1`, which stops git's helper
  chain. The fallback secret is never read then, so a broker outage cannot swap identity on
  our own repositories.
- Owner missing from the release but in the covered-owner file: refuse. A release that drops
  an owner it covered before is a contradiction, not permission to act as the human.
- Owner never covered: answer with the profile's fallback secret (the user's personal access
  token for upstream repositories the App is not installed on). No fallback-secret
  configured, or the secret unreadable: `quit=1` naming the cause. Nothing ever falls through
  to the next helper or to the user's keyring login.
"""

